    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "Where life finds a home"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "Penguins in the cold, camels in the desert, fish in the ocean: each lives where its body suits the place. Landforms and climate decide where life flourishes."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "adaptation",
          "biodiversity",
          "biomes",
          "biosphere",
          "conservation",
          "human adaptation",
          "interconnected",
          "physiography and climate"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "Physiography, meaning the landforms and water bodies of a region, and climate together shape life on Earth.\nThe biosphere is the narrow zone where land, water and air meet and support life.\nLarge regions with similar climate and life form biomes, such as forests, grasslands, deserts and tundra.\nOrganisms have adaptations, features that help them survive in their habitat, such as the camel's hump for desert life.\nHumans and other life forms are interconnected through food chains, air, water and soil.\nHumans adapt too, with houses, clothes and farming suited to their region.\nBiodiversity, the variety of life, is important for food, medicine and balance in nature.\nWe conserve it through protected areas, afforestation and responsible use."
        }
      },
